    "ls|LS17_S12_U_172": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 172\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nSECURITY CHECK DURING AND POST COVID-19\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether security checking of the passengers was being carried out with\nscanning machines at all the airports during Covid-19 to avoid touching the body of\npassengers;\n \n(b) if so, the details of the total number of body scanning machines installed at the\nairports along with their total cost and the number of them operational at present;\n \n(c) whether security checking of the passengers is again being carried out manually\nsince 2021 as a result of which the bodies of security personnel and the passengers\ncome in contact with each other which may lead to various kind of infections and if\nso, the details thereof;\n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to resort to touch less body security scanning\nsystem of the passengers through scanning machines installed at all the airports;\n \n(e) if so, the time likely to be done; and\n \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (f): Yes, Sir. The Pat Down search method was kept in abeyance during\nCovid-19 to contain the spread by touch/ contact. The security checking/screening\nof the passengers at airports is carried out with the help of the Door Frame Metal\nDetector (DFMD), Hand Held Metal Detector (HHMD) and if required, by pat-\ndown search methods. Presently, there is no plan to do away with Pat Down search\nmethod. \n \n*****\n172. SHRI GIRIDHARI YADAV\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_173": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  \n \n LOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *173  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.08.2023  \n \nFlagship Programme for Adolescents and Youth  \n \n*173. SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has sta rted any flagship programme \nfor the development of adolescents and youth of the country;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the objectives, features and \nfinancial allocation for the said programme; and   \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor?  \n  \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS  \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR]  \n  \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the table of the House.  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *173 FOR REPLY ON 01.08.2023 \nASKED BY SHRI RAJENDRA DHEDYA GAVIT REGARDING FLAGSHIP \nPROGRAMME FOR ADOLESCENTS AND YOUTH.  \n  \n(a) Yes, the Government runs a scheme called National Programme \nfor Youth and Adolescent Development (NPYAD) as part of a flagship \nprogramme viz., Rashtriya Yuva Sashaktikaran Karyakram (RYSK).  \n   \n(b) The objectives of the NPYAD scheme is to engage youth for \nnation building; promote spirit of National  Integration; stimulate \naction for development and empowerment of adolescent; develop \namongst youth a sense of pride in nationally accepted values like \ndemocracy, socialism, and secularism; foster social harmony and \nnational unity among youth; motivate you th to act as focal point for \ndissemination of knowledge in the rural area and involve them in \nnation building process. Under NPYAD Scheme financial assistance \nis provided to Government / Non-Government Organisations for taking \nup activities for youth and adolescent development under following 5 \nprogramme areas:  \n  \ni. Youth Leadership and Personality Development Training.  \nii. Promotion of National Integration (National Integration \nCamps, Inter -State Youth Exchange Programmes, Youth  \nFestivals, National Youth Award, etc.)  \niii. Promotion of Adventure; Tenzing No rgay National \nAdventure Awards. \niv. Development and Empowerment of Adolescents (Life \nSkills Education, Counselling, Career Guidance, etc.).  \nv. Technical and Resource Development (Research and \nStudies on Youth issues, Documentation, Seminars/ \nWorkshops).  \n  \nThe major highlights/features of NPYAD Scheme are organization of \nNational Youth Festival, North East Youth Festival and Youth Pravasi \nBharatiya Divas and conferment of National Youth Award and Tenzing \nNorgay National Adventure Award.  \n \n  \n\nFinancial Allocation under NPYAD Scheme in the last three years are:  \n  \n(Rs. In crore)  \n Year BE  RE  Actuals  \n2020-21  23.00  20.00  12.79  \n2021-22  21.00  25.15  15.10  \n2022-23  22.00  26.35  18.68  \n  \n(c) Does not arise.  \n  \n*****  \n  \n  \n  \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_174": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 174\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nPRICE RISE IN AIRFARE\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether there has been a sharp rise in the prices of airline tickets in the last few\nmonths which has made it difficult for people to afford air travel and if so, the details\nthereof;\n \n(b) whether the Government has any scheme to make air travel accessible and affordable\nto all;\n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and\n \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (d) Airfares are market driven and are neither regulated nor established by the\nGovernment. Airlines are free to fix reasonable tariffs taking into consideration all\nrelevant factors which include the cost of operation, characteristics of service and\nreasonable profit. Airlines, however, remain compliant with the regulatory provisions\nunder Aircraft Rules, 1937 as long as the fare charged by them does not exceed the fare\nestablished and displayed on their website.\n \nDirectorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has established Tariff Monitoring Unit\nwhich monitors fares on select sectors on random basis. This ensures that the airfares\ncharged by the airlines are within the established tariff of the airlines, which is displayed\non their website. Increase in airfare is primarily driven by seasonality, and demand-\nsupply constraints and is also attributable to increase in fuel prices. Government has\ntaken steps to sensitise the airlines. The airfares in these select sectors are monitored on\ndaily basis and as a result of which they have shown a declining trend.\n \nMinistry of Civil Aviation has launched Regional Connectivity Scheme (RCS) - UDAN\n(Ude Desh ka Aam Nagrik) on 21.10.2016 to stimulate regional air connectivity and\nmaking air travel affordable to the masses. The scheme has been able to provide air\nconnectivity to Tier-2 &Tier-3 cities across the country at affordable airfares and has\ntransformed the way people travel.\n \nUnder the UDAN Scheme, the Government has targeted an indicative airfare at Rs. 2500\n174.  KUNWAR DANISH ALI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))\n\nfor a distance of 500 km to 600 km per RCS seat under Regional Connectivity routes\n(equivalent to one-hour flight). Selected Airline Operator is required to provide up to\n50% of the aircraft seating capacity or maximum 40 seats, whichever is lower, as Seats in\na fixed wing aircraft under UDAN. This airfare is subject to indexation as per the\nformula specified in the UDAN Scheme document.\n \n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_175": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 175 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2023 \n \nSCHEME FOR UTILIZATION OF BIOMASS \n175. SH. SUNIL KUMAR MONDAL \nWill the Minister of NEW AND RENEWABLE ENERGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to launch any scheme under which the utilization of \nhuge surplus biomass could be done because of its large availability in West Bengal and other \nparts of the country; \n(b) if so, the details along with the future initiatives thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF NEW & RENEWABLE ENERGY AND POWER  \n(SHRI R.K. SINGH) \n(a) to(c) The Government has taken various steps to promote the utilization of surplus biomass \nacross the country including the State of West Bengal which, inter alia, include the following: \n (i) The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy has notified National Bioenergy Programme in \nNovember, 2022 for the period 01.04.2021 to 31.03.2026 . This programme, with a budgetary \noutlay of Rs. 1715 Crore, shall be implemented in two phases. The first phase has a budgetary \noutlay of Rs. 858 Crore. This programme provides Central Financial Assistance for setting up of \nBioenergy plants. \n(ii) The Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas has launched the Sustainable Alternative Towards \nAffordable Transportation (SATAT) initiative in October, 2018 for offtake of \nBioCNG/Compressed Biogas (CBG) after purification through Oil Marketing Companies \n(OMCs) for sale as automotive fuels. \n (iii)  Departm ent of Drinking Water and Sanit ation, Ministry of Jal Shakti,  has notified  \nGOBARDHAN scheme wherein financial assistance is available for setting up of community \nbiogas plants in villages, blocks / district. \n(iv) Ministry of Power has notified SAMARTH mission (National Mission on Use of Biomass in \nThermal Power Plants) for promoting the blending of biomass in the existing coal powered \nthermal power plants.   \n          Apart from above programmes/schemes,  Central Pollution Control Board, Ministry of \nEnvironment, Forest and Climate Change has notified guidelines to provide financial support for \nsetting up of Biomass Pellet plants in the NCT of Delhi, States of Punjab and Haryana and NCR \ndistricts of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh. \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_176": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 176 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20 JULY, 2023 \n \nCurtailing the Rising Price of Petroleum Products \n†176.  SHRI KAUSHALENDRA KUMAR:  \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has devised any scheme for curtailing rising price of petroleum \nproducts in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor;  \n(c) whether the Government has substantially raised the price of LPG compelling those living in  \nrural areas to get back to the firewoods for cooking; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the number of LPG consumers as on date against the \nnumber thereof in May, 2016 at the time of launch of Pradhan Mantri Ujjawala Yojana in the \nrural areas? \nANSWER \n \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ालय  म\u0000 रा\u0000य  मं\u0000ी  \n(\u0000ी  रामे\u0000 वर तेल\u0000) \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n (SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d): Prices of petrol and diesel have been market-determined with effect from \n26.06.2010 and 19.10.2014 respectively. Since then, the Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies \n(OMCs) take appropriate decision on pricing of petrol and diesel.  \nCentral Government reduced Central Excise duty on petrol and diesel by a total of Rs. 13/ litre \nand Rs. 16/ litre respectively in two tranches in November 2021 and May 2022. The excise \nreductions were fully passed on to consumers and retail prices fell as a result. The measure was \naimed to give a further fillip to the economy and to boost consumption and keep inflation low, \nthus helping the poor and middle classes. Many States/UTs also reduced the VAT on petrol and \ndiesel subsequently. \n\nIndia imports more than 60% of its domestic LPG consumption. Government continues to \nmodulate the effective price of domestic LPG. Under the Direct Benefit Transfer for LPG \n(DBTL) scheme, subsidy as admissible, is deposited in the account of eligible beneficiaries. In \naddition, w.e.f. 21st May, 2022, Government has started a targeted subsidy of Rs. 200 per 14.2 \nKg cylinder for Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) beneficiaries for upto 12 refills a year \nfor years 2022-23 and 2023-24.  \nPrice of domestic LPG (at Delhi) was Rs. 1053 w.e.f. 6 th July 2022 and is Rs. 1103 in July 2023. \nFor PMUY consumers effective price is Rs. 903 after a targeted subsidy of Rs. 200 per cylinder. \n \nThe consumption of LPG in the country has gone up from 19.62 Million Metric Tonnes (MMT) \nin 2015-16 to 28.50 MMT in FY 2022-23. Also, the number of domestic LPG consumers has \nincreased from 16.67 crore as on 01.05.2016 to 31.5 crore (including 9.59 crore PMUY \nbeneficiaries) as on 01.07.2023. \nx-x-x-x-x \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_177": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER AND SANITATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †177 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2023 \n \nNAL SE JAL YOJANA \n \n†177. SHRI DURGA DAS (D.D.) UIKEY: \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether any action plan is being prepared by the Ministry for proper implementation of Nal Se \nJal Yojana to supply water in tribal and hilly areas particularly in Shahpur, Bhimpur, Bhaisdehi, \nTimrahi blocks of Betul Parliamentary Constituency; and \n \n(b) if so, the details of the status of water supply in the next ten years under this yojana in the said \nareas? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b) Government of India, in partnership with States/UTs is implementing Jal Jeevan Mission \n(JJM), since August 2019, to make provision of tap water supply to every rural household in the \ncountry including those in Shahpur, Bhimpur, Bhaisdehi, Timrahi blocks of Betul Parliamentary \nConstituency of Madhya Pradesh. \n \n   At the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission in Madhya Pradesh, 13.53 lakh \n(11.28%) rural households were reported to have tap water connections. Since then, tap water \nconnections have been provided to additional 47.51 lakh rural households. As on 18.07.2023, out \nof total 119.65 lakh rural households in Madhya Pradesh, provision of tap water supply has been \nmade to 61.04 lakh (51.02) households. Similarly, as on 18.07.2023 out of 2.73 lakh households of \nBetul district of Madhya Pradesh, 1. 86 Lakhs rural households have been provided tap water \nconnections.  \n The States/UTs, districts & villages-wise status of tap water connection in rural households, \nindicating their status of coverage, etc. including those in Shahpur, Bhimpur, Bhaisdehi, Timrahi \nblocks of Betul Parliamentary Constituency of Madhya Pradesh as reported by States/ UTs is in \npublic domain and available on JJM dashboard at \nhttps://ejalshakti.gov.in/jjmreport/JJMIndia.aspx.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_178": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 178\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nUDAN SCHEME FOR ODISHA\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) the details of ongoing and completed projects under the Regional\nConnectivity Scheme - Ude Desh Ka Aam Nagarik (RCS-Udan) in Odisha;\n(b) whether the Union Government proposes to launch new projects under\nRCS-UDAN in Odisha; and\n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of State in the Ministry of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a)  Airstrips at Amarda, Jeypore, Jharsuguda, Rangeilunda, Rourkela and\nUtkela in Odisha have been  identified for development under UDAN and to\noperate flights under this scheme. Flight operations have commenced from\nJharsuguda, Jeypore and Rourkela airports after the revival of these airports.\nThe remaining  airstrips  are under various stages of development\n \n(b) & (c) : UDAN is a market driven scheme. Based on their assessment of\ndemand on particular routes, interested airlines submit their proposals at the\ntime of bidding under UDAN. An airport which is included in the awarded\nroutes of UDAN and requires upgradation/development for commencement of\nUDAN operations, is developed under 'Revival of unserved and underserved\nairports' scheme.\nThe Government has released the fifth version of UDAN on 21.04.2023 to\nprovide air connectivity to underserved and unserved  destinations of the\ncountry, including the state of Odisha. \n \n*****\n178. SHRI RAMESH CHANDRA MAJHI\n be pleased to state:-\n (GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD))",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_179": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 179 \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2023 \nDRYING UP OF DAMS AND RESERVOIRS \n179.   SHRI PRAJWAL REVANNA  \n Will the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government is aware of drying up of dams and reservoirs in the State of Karnataka due \nto delayed monsoon; \n(b) if so, the details of the current water level and storage capacity of the major dams and reservoirs in \nthe State in comparison to previous year water level as on date; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to desilting of dams and reservoirs in the State owing to less \nwater storage despite good monsoon in the previous year and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) whether the Government is aware of water scarcity in northern Karnataka due to drying up of \nKrishna river and if so, the action taken by the Government to release more water from Maharashtra to \nmitigate crisis situation in the State? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI BISHWESWAR TUDU) \n(a) & (b)  Central Water Commission monitors live storage status of 146 important reservoirs in the \ncountry on weekly basis and issues weekly bulletin on every Thursday. Out of 146 numbers of \nreservoirs monitored by CWC, 16 belongs to Karnataka. The details of live storage of these 16 \nimportant reservoirs in comparison with live storage of previous year are given in Annexure. \n(c)   Government of India has taken up the externally funded Dam Rehabilitation and Improvement \nProject (DRIP), Phase II & III (2021-2031) scheme envisaging rehabilitation and safety improvement of \n736 dams located in 19 States, with a budget outlay of Rs. 10,211 crores. Further, State of Karnataka is \nparticipating in this scheme. 41 dams of Karnataka are included for rehabilitation under the scheme at a \nbudget outlay of Rs. 612 crores. Under this scheme, there is a need-based provision of de-silting of \nselected reservoirs.  \n(d)   The details of live storage of important reservoirs of North Karnataka in comparison with live \nstorage of previous year are also given in Annexure. Further, as informed by the State of Karnataka, \nthey have requested Government of Maharashtra for release of 3 TMC of water from Warna/Koyna \nreservoir to Krishna river and 3 TMC of water from Ujjini reservoir to Bhima river to meet the drinking \nwater needs for month of April, May & June 2023. Responding to the request of Karnataka, \nGovernment of Maharashtra has released 1 TMC of water for drinking needs in the month of May 2023. \n  \n  \n  \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 179  TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 20.07.2023 REGARDING “DRYING UP OF \nDAMS AND RESERVOIRS”. \n  \n \n*****  \n  \n   \n  \n \n \n \n \nS. NO \n \n \n \nNAME OF RESERVOIR \n \n \nFRL \n(m) \n \nCURRENT \nRESERVO\nIR LEVEL \n(m) \n \nLIVE \nCAPACIT\nY AT FRL \n(BCM) \n \nCURREN\nT LIVE \nSTORAG\nE (BCM) \n \n \n \nDATE \nSTORAGE AS % OF LIVE \nCAPACITY AT FRL \n \nCURRE\nNT \nYEAR \n \nLAST \nYEAR \nLAST 10 YEARS \nAVERA GE \n1 KRISHNARAJA SAGARA 752.50 741.43 1.163 0.189  13-07-2023 16 93 49 \n2 TUNGABHADRA 497.74 483.18 3.276 0.212  13-07-2023 6 86 36 \n3 GHATAPRABHA(HIDKAL) 662.95 636.13 1.391 0.114  13-07-2023 8 35 29 \n4 BHADRA 657.76 644.07 1.785 0.542  13-07-2023 30 89 44 \n5 LINGANAMAKKI 554.43 533.99 4.294 0.564  12-07-2023 13 39 30 \n6 NARAYANPUR 492.25 486.83 0.863 0.195  13-07-2023 23 72 46 \n7 MALAPRABHA(RENUKA) 633.83 622.72 0.972 0.095  13-07-2023 10 38 20 \n8 KABINI 696.66 691.66 0.444 0.211  13-07-2023 48 92 63 \n9 HEMAVATHY 890.63 881.82 0.927 0.324  13-07-2023 35 96 53 \n10 HARANGI 871.42 865.90 0.220 0.099  13-07-2023 45 79 68 \n11 SUPA 564.00 527.25 4.120 0.938  12-07-2023 23 30 33 \n12 VANI VILAS SAGAR 652.28 650.25 0.802 0.650  13-07-2023 81 73 15 \n13 ALMATTI 519.60 508.22 3.105 0.283  13-07-2023 9 70 50 \n14 GERUSOPPA 55.00 46.91 0.130 0.087  13-07-2023 67 81 78 \n15 MANI DAM 594.36 573.48 0.884 0.124  13-07-2023 14 31 26 \n16 TATTIHALLA 468.30 449.75 0.249 0.001  12-07-2023 0 27 9 ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_18": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 18 \nANSWERED ON 20th July, 2023  \n \nROAD CONNECTIVITY IN TELANGANA AND ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n18.                    SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY:         \n  \n       Will the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has dedicated various  National Highway \nprojects for improving road connectivity of both Telangana and Andhra \nPradesh and also to other connecting States; \n \n(b) if so, the details of surveys/feasibility reports; \n \n(c) the funds sanctioned/spent on the same and progress made so far; \n \n(d) the actual demands of roads by the various  States  considered/ \nrejected along with the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) the details of plan of action proposed to meet  the future needs of \nvarious States in construction of NHs?  \n  \n \n \n \n  \n\nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n  \n(Shri NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n  \n(a), (b) & (c)  Ministry has taken up  various National Highway \nprojects for improving road connectivity of both Telangana and Andhra \nPradesh and also to other connecting States  viz., Tamil Nadu, \nKarnataka, Maharashtra, Chhattisgarh and  Odisha. The details of such \nprojects, sanctioned based on survey/feasibility/technical reports \nduring the last 3 financial years (i.e., F.Y. 2020-21, 2021-22 and 2022-23) \nincluding funds sanctioned for these  projects and the progress of \nexpenditure are at Annexure. \n    \n(d) & (e)   Development and maintenance of National Highways \n(NHs) is a continuous process . Projects to be taken up in successive \nyears are finalized based on the outcome of DPRs, traffic volume, inter-\nse-priority and availability of resources. The demands for development \nof NHs received from the states are considered based on the approved \nAnnual Plans.  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a), (b) and (c)  OF THE \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 18 FOR ANSWER ON \n20.07.2023 ASKED BY SHRI KOTHA PRABHAKAR REDDY  REGARDING \n“ROAD CONNECTIVITY IN TELANGANA AND ANDHRA PRADESH” \n  \nDetails for various National Highway projects dedicated for road \nconnectivity of both Telangana and Andhra Pradesh and also to oth er \nconnecting States i.e. Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Maharashtra, \nChhattisgarh and Odisha. \n \n \nSr No.  State No of Works Total Sanctioned \nCost (Rs. In Crore)  Length (Km) \nTotal Expenditure \nupto 30.06.2023 \n(Civil Work) Rs. In \nCrore  \n1 Telangana 71 37069 1958 2580 \n2 Andhra \nPradesh 96 37220 2586 5736 \n3 Odisha 19 2204 204 222 \n4 Chhattisgarh 1 37 36 26 \n5 Maharashtra 3 504 83 258 \n\n6 Karnataka 12 5282 400 138 \n7 Tamil Nadu 6 446 24 31 \n \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_180": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 180\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2023\n \n \nDEVELOPMENT OF KHADI VILLAGE INDUSTRIES\n \n \n180.\n \n \nSHRIMATI RANJEETA KOLI: \n \nDR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \n \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND \nSARASWATI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the development of Khadi and Village Industries sector has been taking place at \nrapid pace during the last three years; \n \n(b) \n \nif\n \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(c) \n \nthe extent of rise in the consumption of Khadi products in the country during the said \nperiod; (d) \n \nthe details of the Khadi products and the domestic and foreign revenue earned \ntherefrom, product and district\n-\nwise; and \n \n(e)\n \n \nthe steps being taken by the Government to promote the export of Khadi products?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \n \nYes Sir, the development of Khadi and Village Industries (KVI\n) sector by Khadi and \nVillage Industries Commission (KVIC) has been taking place at rapid pace through the \nimplementation of various schemes and programmes like Khadi Gramodyog Vikas Yojana, Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme, etc. The detail\ns of performance of these \nschemes/programmes during the last three years are as follows:\n \n \ni\n.\n \nPrime Minister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP):\n \nDuring last three \nyears 2,62,801 number of micro enterprises assisted by disbursement of Margin Money \nSubsid\ny of Rs.7,88,860.42 lakh and estimated employment to 21,02,408 persons have been \ngenerated.\n \nii\n.\n \nModified Market Development Assistance (MMDA):\n \nDuring last three years, MMDA \nto the tune of Rs. 674.40 crore has been disbursed to Khadi artisans and Khadi\n \nInstitutions \n(KIs).\n \niii\n.\n \nInterest Subsidy Eligibility Certificate (ISEC):\n \nDuring last three years,\n \nRs. 102.12 crore \ninterest subsidy has been paid to KIs.\n \niv\n.\n \nWork\n-\nshed scheme for Khadi Artisan\n:\n \nDuring last three years, 3,430 artisans have been \nassisted under work\n \nshed scheme.\n \nv\n.\n \nStrengthening Infrastructure of Existing Weak Khadi Institutions and Assistance for \nMarketing Infrastructure:\n \nDuring last three years, 66 weak KIs have been assisted and \n185 sales outlets have been renovated.\n \nvi\n.\n \nHoney Mission/ Beekeeping \nProgramme\n: During last three years, 52,080 bee boxes have \nbeen distributed to 5,208 beekeepers.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n: 2 :\n \n \n \n \nvii\n.\n \nKumbhar Sashaktikaran Programme/Pottery Programme:\n \nDuring last three years, \n11,687electric pottery wheels have been distributed to trained potters\n.\n \n \nviii\n.\n \nAgro Based and Food Processing Industry (ABFPI)\n: During last 3 years, 2,120 \nbeneficiaries have been trained under ABFPI.\n \n \nix\n.\n \nAgarbatti Programme under Wellness and Cosmetics Industries\n: During last 2 years, \n1,425 beneficiaries have been assisted under the \ninitiative.\n \n \nx\n.\n \nLeather Footwear Manufacturing and Leather Footwear Repairing programme\n: \nDuring last three years, 1,300 beneficiaries have been trained under Leather Footwear \nIndustry.\n \n \nxi\n.\n \nHandmade Paper and Fibre Industry\n: During last three years, 3,647 benefic\niaries have \nbeen trained under Handmade Paper and Fibre Industry.\n \n \nxii\n.\n \nRural Engineering and New Technology Industries (RENTI): \nSince 2021\n-\n22, 2,260 \nbeneficiaries have been trained under RENTI.\n \n \nxiii\n.\n \nCapacity Building:\n \nsince last three years, 1,\n78,181\n \nnumbers of candidate trained under \nSkill Development Programme and Entrepreneurship Awareness Programme.  Apart from \nthis, 1,67,912 number of entrepreneurs trained under Entrepreneurship Development \nProgramme under Prime Minister’s Employment Generation P\nrogramme (PMEGP).\n \n \nBesides, under \nScheme of Funds for Regeneration of Traditional Industries (SFURTI), \na \ntotal of 513 traditional industry clusters have been approved since 2015\n-\n16 with a GoI assistance of \nRs. 1332.95 cr., covering 3.03 lakh traditional ar\ntisans. These clusters are set up in Khadi, \nHandicrafts, Textiles, Agro\n-\nprocessing, Honey, Coir sector. \n \n \n(c):\n \nThe details of \nSales of Khadi products and the percentage increase from the previous year \nduring last three years are as follows:\n \n \nYear\n \nSales of \nKhadi Products \n \n(Rs. in Crore)\n \nPercentage increase from previous year\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n3527.71\n \n-\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n5051.72\n \n43.20\n \n2022\n-\n23\n \n5942.93\n \n17.64\n \n \n(d):\n   \n \nThe Year\n-\nwise and category\n-\nwise details of the sales of \nKhadi\n \nProducts in domestic market \nduring last three years are as follows:\n \n(Rs. in \nCrore\n)\n \nSales of Khadi Products in the country                      \n \nYear\n \nCotton\n \nWoollen\n \nSilk\n \nPolyvastra\n \nSolarvastra\n \nTotal\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n1740.95\n \n441.93\n \n902.65\n \n436.52\n \n5.66\n \n3527.71\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n2145.05\n \n608.30\n \n1612.71\n \n678.38\n \n7.28\n \n5051.72\n \n2022\n-\n23 \n(Provisional)\n \nUnder compilation\n \n5942.93\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n: 3 :\n \n \n \n \nThe State wise details of Domestic revenue earned during the year 2020\n-\n21 and 2021\n-\n22 by \nSales of Khadi Products is placed at \nAnnexure \n-\n \nI\n.\n \n \nRegarding the foreign revenue earned from the export of Khadi products, KVIC does not \ninvolve itself directly in the export activities, but Khadi institutions export their products directly or \nthrough Merchant/Agencies.\n \nThe State wise details of Khadi prod\nucts exported through \nMerchant/Agencies in 2021\n-\n22 and 2022\n-\n23 is placed at \nAnnexure \n-\n \nII\n. \n \n \n(e):\n \nKVIC does not involve itself directly in the export activities, but Khadi\n \nand Village \nIndustries institutions/units export their products directly or through Merchant/Agencies.\n \nThe steps \ntaken by KVIC to promote the export of KVI products are as follows:\n \ni\n.\n \nKVIC has been granted the status of Deemed Export Promotion Council (EPC) \nstatus by \nMinistry of Commerce & Industry in December, 2006.\n \nii\n.\n \nGovernment has issued HS code bracket to Khadi and Village Industries Commission \n(KVIC) to categorize 11 KVI products in export. \n \niii\n.\n \nKVIC under International Cooperation (IC) Scheme of Ministry of M\nSME facilitates its \nKVI units for participation in international exhibitions/trade fairs, etc. for showcasing \nquality of KVI products.\n \niv\n.\n \nKVIC participates every year in India International Trade Fair (IITF) organized by India \nTrade Promotion Organization (IT\nPO) at Pragati Maidan, New Delhi. The peripheral Brands \nof KVIC and MSME products are displayed every year in the pavilion.\n \nv\n.\n \nKVIC also regularly conducts export related workshops and participates in Fashion Shows \nfor the benefit of KVI institutions/units.  \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n\nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (d) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 180 for \nanswer on 20.07.2023\n \n \nState wise details of Domestic revenue earned during the year 2020\n-\n21 and 2021\n-\n22 by Sales \nof Khadi Products\n \nSr.   \nNo.\n \nStates   /   \nUnion \nTerritories\n \nState\n-\nWise and Category\n-\nWise Khadi   Sales\n-\n2020\n-\n21\n \n(Rs. In Lakh)\n \nCotton\n \nWoollen\n \nSilk\n \nPolyvastra\n \nSolarvastra\n \nTotal\n \n1\n \nJammu & \nKashmir\n \n538.70\n \n4878.63\n \n75.89\n \n22.25\n \n0.00\n \n5515.47\n \n2\n \nHimachal \nPradesh\n \n393.82\n \n156.63\n \n52.15\n \n16.29\n \n0.00\n \n618.89\n \n3\n \nPunjab\n \n1058.88\n \n146.52\n \n330.58\n \n193.11\n \n0.00\n \n1729.09\n \n4\n \nChandigarh \n(UT)\n \n122.94\n \n28.19\n \n146.73\n \n12.18\n 