    "ls|LS17_S12_U_196": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 196 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023 \n \nCREDIT RATING OF CITIES \n \nNO. 196.   SHRI BENNY BEHANAN: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)  the average credit rating calculated for urban cities as per the reforms \nunder AMRUT; \n(b)  the reasons for the integration of Central and State implementation in \nthe working of the AMRUT  scheme along with the details of \nresponsibility structure; \n(c)  the progress made in water connectivity  under the AMRUT Scheme \nduring the last three years; and \n(d)  whether the Government has any explanation for the substantial \ndifference in funding ratios between the completed 4,676 projects worth \nINR 32,793 crores under the AMRUT scheme and the 1,197 projects \nworth INR 49,430 crores that are at various  stages of implementation, \nconsidering the significantly  smaller number of projects in the latter \ncategory and if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n \n(a):   Under Atal Mission Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation ( AMRUT) \nReforms, Credit rating work for 485 cities have been awarded and completed in \n470 cities. Out of 470 cities, 164 cities have received Investible Grade Rating \n(IGR), including 36 cities with rating of A- or above, spread across 12 States/ UTs \nand 306 cities received Non-  Investible Grade Rating (Non- IGR). The details of \ngrade-wise no. of cities are at ANNEXURE.  \n…2/ - \n\n \n \n-2- \n(b): Sanitation, water supply and urban development are State subjects. The \nCentral Government, through various program interventions incl uding AMRUT, \nsupplements the efforts of State/ Union Territories  (UTs) in improving these \nfacilities. The objective of empowering of States integration of central and State \nimplementation in the working of the scheme like AMRUT have been done in \nwhich the centre only approves the State Annual Action Plans (SAAPs) while the \nStates develop, appraise, approve, award and execute the projects.  \n(c): During the last three years, since 01 July 2020, 93 lakh water tap \nconnections have been provided/  serviced under AMRUT Mission and \nConvergence in 500 AMRUT cities. \n(d): 4,676 projects comprise large number of smaller projects worth less than \n₹30 crore while 1,197 projects comprise bigger size of projects.  In ongoing 916 \nprojects about 77% of work has been physically completed.  \n \n******\n\n \n \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 196 REGARDING “CREDIT RATING OF CITIES ”, DUE \nFOR ANSWER IN THE LOK SABHA ON 20 JULY, 2023. \n \nGrade-wise no. of cities for which credit rating has been \ncompleted \nS.No. Credit rating No. of cities \n1 A 6 \n2 A- 9 \n3 A+ 7 \n4 AA 3 \n5 AA- 4 \n6 AA+ 4 \n7 AAA 3 \n8 B 11 \n9 B- 1 \n10 B+ 38 \n11 BB 110 \n12 BB- 92 \n13 BB+ 52 \n14 BBB 46 \n15 BBB- 59 \n16 BBB+ 23 \n17 D 2 \nTotal 470 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_197": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 197 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 02.08.2023 \n \nNEW RAILWAY LINES FROM CHENNAI TO CUDDALORE \n \n*197.    SHRI VE. VAITHILINGAM: \n  \n    Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the present status of new railway lines from Chennai to \nCuddalore via Mahabalipuram, Marakkanam and Puducherry and \nthat connecting Nagari-Tindivanam-Puducherry; \n \n(b) whether the Railways is considering a separate project of \nconnecting Tindivanam and Cuddalore via Puducherry; and  \n \n(c) if so, the plan envisaged for the same? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 197 BY SHRI VE. VAITHILINGAM TO BE ANSWERED IN \nLOK SABHA ON 02.08.2023 REGARDING  NEW RAILWAY LINES FROM \nCHENNAI TO CUDDALORE \n \n(a) to (c): Chennai-Cuddalore via Mamallapuram, Marakanam, \nPuducherry is a new line project of 179.28 Km  length. Latest anticipated \ncost of the project is `2670 crore. An outlay of `50 crore has been \nprovided for Financial Year 2023 -24. Government of Puducherry had \nrequested for change in alignment towards northern an d southern sides \nof Villupuram -Puducherry existing line as well as doubling of tracks \nbetween Puducherry and Cuddalore.   \n \n As aforesaid revision in alignment was based on the request of the \nGovernment of Puducherry, State Government was requested to bear \nadditional cost due to above revision in alignment and doubling of \ntracks.  \n \n However, the State Government has shown their inability to \nbear additional cost of the project due to revision in alignment vide \nletter dated 16.05.2018. However, Final Location Survey (FLS) has been \ntaken up on revised alignment.  \n Tindivanam-Nagari is a new line project of 185 Km length. Latest \nanticipated cost of the project is `3631 crore. An expenditure of `697 \ncrore has been incurred upto March, 2023. The 6 Km section between \nWalajah Road to Ranipet has been commissioned. Further land \nacquisition has been taken up by State Government of Tamil Nad u. An \noutlay of `200 crore has been provided for Financial Year 2023-24. \n...2/- \n \n \n\n \n \n-2- \n \nSurvey of new line between Tindivanam and Puducherry (44 Km) \nwas conducted in 2015 -16. The project could not be taken forward due \nto low traffic projections.  Puducherry to Cuddalore is a part of Chennai - \nCuddalore new line project.   \n \nFurther, as on 01.04.2023, 23 projects (09 new lines, 03 Gauge \nConversion and 11 Doubling) covering total length of 2,848 Km at a cost \nof `35,580 crore falling fully/partly in Tamil Nadu  are under different \nstages of planning/approval/execution, out of which 839 Km length has \nbeen commissioned and a n expenditure of `9,078 crore has been \nincurred upto March 2023.   \n \n \nBudget allocation for Infrastructure Projects and safety works \nfalling fully/partly in the State of Tamil Nadu: \n \nPeriod Average Outlay %age more as compared to average \nallocation during 2009-14 \n2009-14 ` 879 Cr. - \n2014- 23 ` 2,523 Cr. 187% more \n2023-24 `6,080 Cr. 592% more \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_198": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 198\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 20.07.2023\n \n \nSTART\n-\nUP COMPANIES\n \n \n198\n.\n \n    \nSHRIMATI SANGEETA AZAD:  \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether it has come to the notice of the Government that 90 to 95 per cent of startup \ncompanies have failed ; \n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nif so, the percentage of failures among Micro, Small and Medium E\nnterprises;\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether the statistics with the Government corroborate with the findings of these \nstudies on failure of startups; and\n \n \n(d) \n \nif not, the failure rate as per the details available with the Government ?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, \nSMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) to (d)\n:\n \nThe Government with an intent to build a strong ecosystem for nurturing \ninnovation, startups and encouraging private investments in startup ecosystem of the country \nlaunched Startup \nIndia initiatives on 16\nth\n \nJanuary 2016. \n \n \nIn order to meet the objectives of the initiative, the Government unveiled an Action \nPlan for Startup India that laid the foundation of Government support, schemes and \nincentives envisaged to create a vibrant start\nup ecosystem in the country. Sustained \nGovernment efforts in this direction have resulted in increasing the number of recognised \nstartups in India from 442 in 2016 to 92,683 in 2023 (as on 28\nth\n \nFebruary 2023).\n \n \nInformation with respect to the failure of st\nartups is not centrally maintained by the \nGovernment.\n \n \n \n*******\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_199": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n DEPARTMENT OF SPACE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, AUGUST 02, 2023 \n \nSETTING UP OF SPACE CENTRE \n \n*199.  SHRI SAUMITRA KHAN \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the total number of space centres set up across the \ncountry; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to set up an ISRO space \ncentre in West Bengal especially in the Rarh Bengal region \nunder the private sector keeping in mind the interests of \nyoung entrepreneurs and st udents in the field of space \nscience; and \n(c) if so, the details of the facilities likely to be provided to the \nyouths under the said scheme? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC \nGRIEVANCES & PENSIONS AND IN THE PRIME MINISTER’S OFFICE \n(DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n**** \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n**** \n\nSTATEMENT LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA IN REPLY TO \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 199 REGARDING “SETTING UP OF SPACE \nCENTRE” ASKED BY SHRI SAUMITRA KHAN FOR ANSWERING ON \nWEDNESDAY, AUGUST 02, 2023. \n \n(a) Department of Space has 31 space centres across the country, \ncomprising of Centres/Units of Indian Space Research \nOrganisation, Autonomous Bodies and Public Sector \nEnterprieses. \n(b) & (c) \nRegional Remote Sensing Centre (RRSC) [East] is operational \nin Kolkata, West Bengal in order to meet the various Remote \nSensing Tasks specific to the eastern region. \nKeeping in mind, the interests of young entrepreneurs and \nstudents in the field of space science, ISRO has set up the \nRegional Academic Centres for Space (RAC -S) at 6 locations \nacross the country. \nRAC-S for the eastern zone has been set up at NIT -Patna, \nwhich also caters to the requirements of West Bengal region \nas well. \nRAC-S enables advanced research in the areas of releva nce to \nthe future technological and programmatic needs of the Indian \nSpace Programme and acts as a facilitator for the promotion of \nspace technology activities in the region. \nAlso, under the Sponsored Research (RESPOND) programme of \nISRO, projects are take n up by Universities/academic \ninstitutions across the country, including West Bengal region, \nin the areas of relevance to space programme.  \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_2": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 2\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 20.07.2023\n \n \nCHALLENGES FACED BY MSMEs\n \n \n \n2\n. \n     \n \nDR. PRITAM GOPINATHRAO MUNDE:\n \nSHRI RAHUL RAMESH SHEWALE:\n \nSHRI CHANDRA SEKHAR SAHU:\n \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES be pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the MSMEs are facing challenges such as formal credit, employment generation, \ndirect selling to the Government, quality manufacturing, micro\n-\ncredit access, \nlack of awareness \nand more;\n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof and the reaction of the Union Government thereto;\n \n(c) \n \nthe steps taken by the Union Government to address the concerns of the MSMEs, \nparticularly in Odisha; and\n \n(d) \n \nthe steps proposed by the Unio\nn Government to meet the challenges of MSMEs to achieve \nthe goal of five trillion economy of the country by 2026?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, SMALL AND MEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a) \nto (c)\n:\n \n \nIn order to help the MSMEs to overcome the challenges such as formal credit, \nemployment generation, direct selling to the Government, quality manufacturing, micro\n-\ncredit \naccess, lack of awareness faced by them, Government of India has taken several measure\ns across \nthe country, including in the State of Odisha which inter alia include Prime Minister’s \nEmployment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Micro and Small Enterprises\n-\nCluster \nDevelopment Programme (MSE\n-\nCDP), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and Small Enter\nprises \n(CGTMSE), Raising and Accelerating MSME Performance (RAMP) scheme, Self Reliant India \n(SRI) Fund Scheme, Procurement and Marketing Support Scheme, etc. \n \n \n(d)\n:\n \nThe Government of India has taken number of steps to meet the challenges of MSMEs to \nachie\nve the goal of five trillion economy of the country. Some of these steps include Launched the \nChampions 2.0 on 27.06.2023 for MSMEs, Self Reliant India (SRI) Fund for MSMEs, MSME \nChampion Scheme for providing assistance and subsidy to MSME, \nBudget 2023\n-\n24 announced \nthe infusion of Rs. 9,000 crore in the corpus of Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro & Small \nEnterprises to enable an additional credit of Rs. 2.00 lakh crore with reduced cost of credit, \nCentral Sector Scheme “Raising and Accele\nrating MSME Performance (RAMP)” aims at \nstrengthening Institutions and governance of MSME programme at the Centre and States, and \nincreasing MSME sector’s access to credit and market, and increasing firm capabilities through \ntechnology upgradation, greenin\ng initiatives, reducing incidence of delayed payments, \nAmendment in Section 43B of the Income Tax Act, 1961 (“the Act”) vide Finance Act 2023, etc. \n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_20": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 20\n(To be answered on the 20th July 2023)\n \nINCREASE IN AIR FARES BY AIRLINES\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n(a) whether it has come to the notice of the Government that the domestic and\ninternational air fares have been increased by all the private airlines and if so, the details\nthereof; and\n(b) the steps taken by the Government to bring a legislation for uniform fares for air\ntravel?\n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) & (b) A STATEMENT IS LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE LOK SABHA.\n \n*****\n*20. SHRI E.T. MOHAMMED BASHEER\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nStatement referred in reply to Part (a) & (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Ques. No. 20\nregarding \"Increase in air fares by Airlines\" to be answered on 20.07.2023.\n \n(a) & (b) Airfares are market driven and are neither regulated nor established by the\nGovernment. Airlines are free to fix reasonable tariffs taking into consideration all\nrelevant factors which include the cost of operation, characteristics of service and\nreasonable profit. Airlines, however, remain compliant with the regulatory provisions\nunder Aircraft Rules, 1937 as long as the fare charged by them does not exceed the fare\nestablished and displayed on their website.\n \nDirectorate General of Civil Aviation (DGCA) has established Tariff Monitoring Unit\nwhich monitors fares on select sectors on random basis. This ensures that the airfares\ncharged by the airlines are within the established tariff of the airlines, which is displayed\non their website. The recent increase in airfare has been noticed in a few sectors,\nprimarily driven by seasonality and demand-supply constraints. Further, the increase in\nairfare is also attributable to increase in fuel prices. Government has taken steps to\nsensitize the airlines. The airfares in these select sectors are monitored on daily basis and\nas a result of which they have shown a declining trend.\n \nThere is no proposal at present to interfere with the existing regulatory framework on\nairfare.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_200": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 200  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023 \n \nHYGIENIC FOOD STREETS \n \nNO. 200.  SHRI Y. DEVENDRAPPA: \n  SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \n  SHRI DEVJI M. PATEL: \n  SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \n  SHRI DILIP SAIKIA: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \na) whether the Government has decided to develop hygienic food streets \nacross the country particularly in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra and \nNorth-Eastern States; \nb) if so, the details thereof along with the salient features of the proposal; \nand \nc) the steps taken/ being taken by the Government to ensure that vendors \nare able to work in a healthy environment without harassment by the \npolice, municipal authorities, etc.? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a)&(b): Ministry of Health & Family Welfare (M/o H&FW) alongwith Ministry \nof Housing & Urban Affairs  (M/o HUA)  have decided to develop 100 \nhealthy & hygienic food streets across the country,  as a pilot with an \naim to encourage safe and healthy food practices among community \nmembers, thus, reducing foodborne illnesses and improving the overall \nhealth outcomes. Under this project, National Health Mission (NHM) of \nM/o H&FW will provide Rs. 1 crore financial  assistance to each food \nstreet and technical support will be provided by Food Safety and \nStandards Authority of India (FSSAI).  The number of hygienic food \nstreets to be developed in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra and North-\nEastern States is at Annexure-I. \n…2/ - \n\n-2- \n (c): In order to protect rights of urban street vendors and to regulate street \nvending activities, the Government has already enacted the Street \nVendors (Protection of Livelihood and Regulation of Street Vending) \nAct, 2014 which is implemented by respective States/UTs by framing \nRules, Scheme, Bye-laws and Plan for Street Vending as per provisions \nof the Act.  \n \n Effective monitoring through issuance of letters, advisories, video \nconferencing, National / Regional workshops etc are being done with \nthe States / UTs for proper implementation of the provisions of the Act. \n \n****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION \nNO. 200 TO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023. \n \nNumber of hygienic food streets to be developed in Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra \nand North-Eastern States \nS. No. State/ Union Territory Numbers of food \nstreets \n1 Gujarat 4 \n2 Rajasthan 4 \n3 Maharashtra 4 \n4 Arunachal Pradesh 1 \n5 Assam 4 \n6 Manipur 1 \n7 Meghalaya 1 \n8 Mizoram 1 \n9 Nagaland  1 \n10 Sikkim 1 \n11 Tripura 1 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_201": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT \nOF \nINDIA\n \nMINISTRY \nOF \nMICRO, SMALL \nAND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 201\n \nTO BE \nANSWERED \nON\n \n20.07.2023\n \n \nUDYAMI BHARAT \n–\n \nMSME DAY\n \n \n201. \n \nSHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR:\n \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL:\n \nDR. SUBHASH RAMRAO BHAMRE:\n \nDR. \nAMOL RAMSING KOLHE:\n \nSHRI SELVAM G.:\n \nSHRI C.N. ANNADURAI:\n \nSHRI KULDEEP RAI SHARMA:\n \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE:\n \n \n \nWill \nthe \nMinister \nof \nMICRO, SMALL \nAND \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES \nbe pleased to \nstate:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government has celebrated Udyami\n \nBharat\n-\nMSME day recently and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the details of programme organised;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) have been consistently registering a \nslower growth rate than the overall growth of the industrial se\nctor in the country, particularly in the \nStates/UTs of Odisha, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu and Andaman & Nicobar Islands and if so, the \ndetails thereof and the reasons therefor;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nwhether any centrally funded schemes are available to improve growth of MSME in th\ne said \nStates/UTs;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nif so, the details of the growth percentage of the MSME sector during the last three years in \nthe said States/UTs, year\n-\nwise;\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nwhether the Government has fixed any target for increasing total number of entrepreneurs in \nMSME sector in the \nsaid States/UTs during the last three years and the current year and if so, the \ndetails thereof; and\n \n(\nf\n)\n \nthe steps taken/proposed to be taken by the Government to achieve the growth targets fixed \nfor the next three years?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MICRO, \nSMALL \nAND\n \nMEDIUM ENTERPRISES\n \n(SHRI BHANU PRATAP SINGH VERMA)\n \n \n(a):\n \nYes, Udyami Bharat\n-\nMSME day was celebrated on 27\nth\n \nJune, 2023 at New Delhi. The \nprogramme includes launch of CHAMPIONS 2.0 Portal, Declaration of results of MSME Idea \nHackathon\n \n2.0, Launch of MSME Idea Hackathon 3.0 for women entrepreneurs, Launch of Mobile \nApp for Geo\n-\ntagging of Cluster Projects and Technology Centers and Releasing of margin money \nsubsidy to PMEGP beneficiaries. On the same day, MoUs have also been signed with \nSIDBI, GEM, \nIndustries Department, Govt. of Tripura, CGTMSE, NSTFDC, NSFDC, NSIC and LG Electronics \nand Electronics Sector Skill Council of India.\n \n \n \n\n: 2 :\n \n \n(b) & (d):\n \nAs per the latest information received from Ministry of Statistics & Programme \nImplementa\ntion, the share of manufacturing MSME Gross Value Output (GVO) in All India \nManufacturing Gross Value Output (GVO) (in %) was as follows:\n \n \nYear\n \n \nShare of MSME manufacturing output in All \nIndia manufacturing output (in %)\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n36.90%\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \n36.60%\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n36.90%\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n36.20%\n \n \n(c):\n \nThe Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises implements various schemes for the \ngrowth and development of MSME sector in the country in areas of credit support, new enterprise \ndevelopment, formalization, \ntechnological assistance, infrastructure development, skill development \nand training and market assistance to MSMEs. The schemes/programmes inter alia include Prime \nMinister’s Employment Generation Programme (PMEGP), Credit Guarantee Scheme for Micro and \nS\nmall Enterprises (CGTMSE), Micro and Small Enterprises\n-\nCluster Development Programme \n(MSE\n-\nCDP), A Scheme for Promoting Innovation, Rural Industry & Entrepreneurship (ASPIRE), \nEntrepreneurship Skill Development Programme (ESDP), Procurement and Marketing Su\npport \nScheme (PMS) and National SC/ST Hub (NSSH).\n \n \nThe Government has taken a number of recent initiatives to suppor\nt MSMEs in the country, \ninclude:\n \n(i)\n \nRs. 5 lakh crore Emergency Credit Line Guarantee Scheme (ECLGS) for business, \nincluding MSMEs.\n \n(ii)\n \nRs.\n \n50,000 crore equity infusion through MSME Self\n-\nReliant India Fund.\n \n(iii)\n \nNew revised criteria for classification of MSMEs.\n \n(iv)\n \nNo global tenders for procurement up to Rs. 200 crores.\n \n(v)\n \nLaunching of an online Portal “Champions” in June, 2020 to cover ma\nny aspects of e\n-\ngovernance including grievance redress and handholding of MSMEs.\n \n(vi)\n \nInclusion of Retail and Wholesale trades as MSMEs w.e.f. 02\nnd\n \nJuly, 2021.\n \n(vii)\n \nNon\n-\ntax benefits extended for 3 years in case of an upward change in status of MSMEs. \n \n(vi\nii)\n \nRoll out of Raising and Accelerating MSME Performance (RAMP) programme with an \noutlay of Rs. 6,000 crore over 5 years.\n \n \n(e) & (f)\n \nThe Udyam Registration, launched on 01.07.2020, is online, free of cost, paperless \nand based on self declaration of inform\nation by the registrants. The enterprise who want to avail the \nbenefits of the Government Schemes/ programmes, may register themselves on the Udyam \nRegistration Portal at the link:\n-\nhttps://udyamregistration.gov.in\n \n \nField offices of Ministry of MSME organiz\ne various awareness campaigns for UDYAM \nregistration and benefits of Government schemes /programmes, in coordination with MSME/ \nIndustry Departments of States/UTs concerned and other MSME stakeholders through physical \nworkshops, social media, print media &\n \nelectronic media for creating wide publicity for increasing \nparticipation from all stakeholders.\n \n \nMinistry of MSME had taken series of special initiatives in the past, through its filed network \nand other stakeholders, to boost the registration of enterpri\nses on Udyam Portal and as a result the \ntotal MSME registration, including Informal Micro Enterprises registered on Udyam Assist Platform \n(UAP), crossed the mark of 2.0 crore.     \n \n***\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_202": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 202 \nANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2023 \n \nNOISE POLLUTION \n \n202.  SHRI MAHABALI SINGH: \nSHRI KHAGEN MURMU: \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA: \n           \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to curb increasing Noise Pollution \nin the country and if so, the details thereof; and \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to allow  music and stop blowing \nof horns in vehicles to check noise pollution? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n(a) The Government has taken various initiatives to curb increasing Noise \nPollution in the country as under:- \n  \n(i) As per sub-rule (2) of rule 119 of Central Motor vehicle Rules, 1989, no \nmotor vehicle, including agricultural tractor and combine harvester, shall \nbe fitted with any multi-toned horn giving a succession of different notes \nor with any other sound producing device giving an unduly harsh, shrill, \nloud or alarming noise.  \n           \nNothing contained in sub -rule (2) shall prevent the use on vehicles \nemployed as ambulance or for fire fighting or salvage  purposes or on \nvehicles used by police officers or operators of construction equipment \nvehicles or officers of the Motor Vehicles Department, in the course of \ntheir duties or on construction equipment vehicles of such sound signals \nas may be approved by the registering authority in whose jurisdiction such \nvehicles are kept. \n\n  \nThe Ministry of Road Transport and Highways, vide letter dated 12th \nJune, 2017 and 11th August, 2017 has issued an advisory to all the State \nGovernments and Union Territories to strictly follow the above mentioned \nprovisions in this regard.  \n  \n(ii) The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways  has issued G.S.R. 953(E) \ndated 05.10.2016 vide which amendment has been made in Form 22 (Road \nWorthiness Certificate for compliance to emission and noise standards) of \nthe Central Motor vehicle Rules, 1989 to assess the noise levels of the \nvehicles. \n  \n(iii)Government of India has notified the Noise Pollution (Regulation and \nControl) Rules, 2000 under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 which \nprescribe Ambient Air Quality Standards in respect of Noise for different \nareas/zones. Government has notified Ambient Air Quality Standards with \nrespect to Noise in Airport Noise Zone. Government has also notified \nNoise standards for firecrackers. \n  \n          For imposing penalty for violation of Noise Pollution (Regulation and \nControl) Rules, 2000, Central Pollution Control Board has prepared \nEnvironment Compensation Regime and circulated it to all State Pollution \nControl Boards (SPCBs) and the Pollution Cont rol Committees (PCCs)  for \nimplementation. \n  \n (b) No such proposal is under consideration with this Ministry. \n \n  \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_203": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 203 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03 AUGUST, 2023 \n \nPrice Hike of Cooking Gas \n†*203.  SHRI SANJAY JADHAV: \n SHRI ARVIND GANPAT SAWANT: \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether inflation has come down recently and if so, the reasons for the price hike of domestic \ngas cylinders and piped gas;  \n(b) whether the prices of LPG commercial cylinders were reduced recently and if so, the reasons \nfor not reducing the prices of domestic cylinders and piped gas;  \n(c) the other measures taken by the Government to provide relief to domestic PNG and CNG \ncustomers; and  \n(d) the details of number of times the prices of LPG commercial cylinders, domestic gas \ncylinders and piped gas increased during the last eight years? \n \n \nANSWER \nपे\u0000ो\u0000लयम  और \u0000ाक ृ \u0000तक गैस मं\u0000ी  \n( \u0000ी  हरद\u0000प \u0000संह पुर\u0000 ) \n \nMINISTER OF PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 203 \nasked by Shri Shri Sanjay Jadhav and Shri Arvind Ganpat Sawant to be answered on 03 \nAugust 2023 regarding “Price Hike of Cooking Gas”. \n \n(a) to (d): India imports more than 60% of its domestic LPG consumption. Prices of LPG in \nthe country are linked to its price in the international market. Prices of commercial LPG are set \nby LPG marketing companies independently. However, for domestic LPG, Government \ncontinues to modulate the effective price to consumer. In addition, Government has started a \ntargeted subsidy of Rs. 200 per 14.2 Kg cylinder for Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana (PMUY) \nbeneficiaries for upto 12 refills a year for years 2022-23 and 2023-24.  \nInflation based on Consumer Price Index-Combined has declined from 7.04% in May 2022 to \n4.31% in May 2023. \nWhile the Retail Selling Prices (RSP) of domestic LPG cylinder remained unchanged at Rs. 1103 \nper 14.2 Kg cylinder for the months of June and July 2023 for Delhi, the RSP of commercial \nLPG cylinder has increased by the Public Sector OMCs from Rs. 1773 in June 2023 to Rs. 1780 \nper 19 Kg cylinder in July 2023 for Delhi.  \nDuring the period 2020-21 to 2022-23, the average Saudi CP (international benchmark for LPG \npricing) went up from $415 per MT to $712 per MT.  However, the increase in international \nprices was not fully passed on in retail prices, due to which the Public Sector Oil Marketing \nCompanies (OMCs) suffered under recoveries of approximately Rs. 28,000 crore on sale of \ndomestic LPG. To compensate this, the Government paid a one-time compensation of Rs. \n22,000 crore to Public Sector OMCs in FY 2022-23.  During Covid pandemic, Government also \nprovided about 14.17 crore free LPG refills to PMUY households under Pradhan Mantri Gareeb \nKalyan Package during 2020. \nThe details of Retail Selling Prices (RSP) of Domestic (14.2 kg) and Commercial (19 kg) LPG \ncylinders at Delhi since April 2015 are given at Annexure-I and Annexure-II respectively. \nGovernment of India allocates Administered Price Mechanism (APM) gas to various City Gas \nDistribution (CGD) entities for CNG (Transport) and PNG (Domestic) purpose. However, retail \nprice of CNG/PNG is determined by CGD entities after considering cost of gas procured, taxes, \ntariff and other components. \nThe government has approved the revised domestic natural gas pricing guidelines vide gazette \nnotification dated 07.04.2023 for APM gas produced from nomination fields of ONGC/OIL, \n\nNew Exploration Licensing Policy (NELP) blocks and pre-NELP blocks, where Production \nSharing Contract (PSC) provides for Government's approval of prices. The price of such natural \ngas has been set as 10% of the monthly average of Indian Crude Basket, be notified on a \nmonthly basis. For the gas produced by ONGC & OIL from their nomination blocks, the \nAdministered Price Mechanism (APM) price is subject to a floor and a ceiling price. These \nreforms have led to significant decrease in prices of Piped Natural Gas (PNG) for household \ncooking and Compressed Natural Gas (CNG) for transport. \nThe details of Domestic Gas (PNG) since April 2015 as per Indraprastha Gas Limited (IGL) at \nDelhi are given at Annexure-III.  \n \nx-x-x-x-x \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 203 asked by Shri Shri Sanjay \nJadhav and Shri Arvind Ganpat Sawant to be answered on 03.08.2023 regarding “Price Hike of \nCooking Gas” \n \nDate Effective From RSP of Domestic LPG at Delhi \n(Rs./14.2Kg cylinder) \n01-Apr-2015  621.00  \n01-May-2015  616.00  \n01-Jun-2015  626.50  \n01-Jul-2015  608.50  \n01-Aug-2015  585.00  \n01-Sep-2015  559.50  \n01-Oct-2015  517.50  \n01-Nov-2015  545.00  \n01-Dec-2015  606.50  \n09-Dec-2015  608.00  \n01-Jan-2016  657.50  \n01-Feb-2016  575.00  \n01-Mar-2016  513.50  \n01-Apr-2016  509.50  \n01-May-2016  527.50  \n01-Jun-2016  548.50  \n01-Jul-2016  537.50  \n01-Aug-2016  487.00  \n01-Sep-2016  466.50  \n01-Oct-2016  490.00  \n28-Oct-2016  492.00  \n01-Nov-2016  529.50  \n01-Dec-2016  584.00  \n01-Jan-2017  585.00  \n01-Feb-2017  651.50  \n01-Mar-2017  737.50  \n01-Apr-17  723.00  \n01-May-17 631.00 \n01-Jun-17 552.50 \n01-Jul-17 564.00 \n01-Aug-17 524.00 \n\nDate Effective From RSP of Domestic LPG at Delhi \n(Rs./14.2Kg cylinder) \n01-Sep-17 597.50 \n30-Sep-17 599.00 \n01-Oct-17 649.00 \n01-Nov-17 742.00 \n01-Dec-17 747.00 \n01-Jan-18 741.00 \n01-Feb-18 736.00 \n01-Mar-18 689.00 \n01-Apr-18 653.50 \n01-May-18 650.50 \n01-Jun-18 698.50 \n01-Jul-18 754.00 \n01-Aug-18 789.50 \n01-Sep-18 820.00 \n01-Oct-18 879.00 \n01-Nov-18 939.00 \n07-Nov-18 942.50 \n01-Dec-18 809.50 \n01-Jan-19 689.00 \n01-Feb-19 659.00 \n01-Mar-19 701.50 \n01-Apr-19 706.50 \n01-May-19 712.50 \n01-Jun-19 737.50 \n01-Jul-19