    "ls|LS17_S12_U_212": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 212 \nANSWERED ON 03RD AUGUST, 2023 \n  \nGREEN HIGHWAYS \n  \n*212. SHRI TAPIR GAO: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n  \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a) the  steps taken by the Ministry to ensure environmental \nsustainability while constructing the National Highways (NHs);  \n  \n(b) the details of the schemes to promote Green Highways in the \ncountry;  \n  \n(c) the total length of NHs constructed in North Eastern States between \n2004-13; and  \n  \n(d) the total length of NHs constructed from 2014 onwards?  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n  \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n   \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 212 FOR ANSWER ON 03 RD AUGUST \n2023 ASKED BY SHRI TAPIR GAO REGARDING GREEN HIGHWAYS. \n  \n(a) & (b) Steps taken by the Government to ensure environment \nsustainability while constructing the National Highways (NHs) as under: \n  \n(i) Ministry of Road Transport & Highways (MoRTH) conducted a \nstudy through The Energy and Resources Institute to estimate \ncarbon emissions from NHs sector and to suggest interventions \nfor making climate resilient highway infrastructure. NHAI has also \npublished the first Sustainability Report as per Global Reporting \nInitiative (GRI) norms, which captures the initiatives of NHAI for \nprotecting and conserving the environment. \n(ii) MoRTH vide Circular No. RH/NH -33044/18/2020-S&R (P&B) \ndated 14.12.2020 has issued the guidelines on use of new / \nalternate materials and technology in construction of National \nHighways. NHAI has also issued policy circular dated 02.06.2022, \nmandating the use of New / Alternative Material and technology in \nconstruction of Highways. \n(iii) Promotion of use of Green Technologies such as : \n• Steel slag in base & sub-base layer  \n• Cement treated sub base \n• Reclaimed asphalt \n• Inert material for landfill  \n• Industrial Waste (Phosphor-gypsum) \n• Construction & Demolition waste \n• Alternate material like stones, gravels and municipal waste  \n• Waste Plastic \n\n(iv) MoRTH vide Circular No. RW/NH -33044/01/2019-S&R (P&B) \nRSCE – Pt.1 dated 18.04.2022 has issued guidelines to use fly ash \nin road/flyover embankment construction on National Highways.  \n(v) Green Highways (Plantation, Transplantation, Beautification \nand Maintenance) Policy, 2015 was launched in September, 2015. \n(vi) Plantation and Transplantation initiatives: \n• Total 373.20 lakh plants planted since 2016 on available RoW.  \n• Total 64,466 mature trees transplanted so far.  \n  \n(vii) Use of renewable energy for operation of Toll Plaza and some \nlocations of Highways by way of installing the solar plate on the \nroof of the toll plaza and vacant place of Highways respectively.   \n(viii) As per MoEF&CC Study “An Assessment of Avoided CO2 Emissions \nDuring Construction, Maintenance and Operation of National Highways”, \nthe saving of natural resources and carbon sequestration from 20 \nstretches/corridors is as under:  \n• Overall consumption of fuel due to the operation of all these \nstretches over a 20-year period is expected to be 19% lower with \nrespect to the BAU situation (a situation when the NH were either \nnot present or were narrower than the present)  \n• Over the same p eriod,  CO2 emissions on these stretches is \nexpected to be 25.19 million tonnes lower. \n• Avenue plantation and compensatory afforestation done post \nconstruction of the highways can sequester 584.27 thousand \ntonne of CO2 in 20 years \n  \n(ix) Amrit Sarovars: \n• Total 433 ‘Amrit Sarovars’ have been constructed along National \nHighways for augmenting the ground water regime of the area \nincluding micro climate of the area. \n  \n\n   (x)  Wildlife Mitigation structure /Passage \n• Construction of wildlife friendly specified struc tures for the \nconservation of wildlife habitat for the stretches passing through \nany protected area under WPA 1972 or any Eco Sensitive Zone/Eco \nFragile areas. \n(c) & (d) The total length of NHs constructed in North Eastern States \nbetween 2004-13 is 2794.455 km and from 2014 onward is 8819 km. \n  \n ***** \n  \n  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_213": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 213 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON JULY 20, 2023 \n  \nRENAMING OF SCHEMES \n \nNO. 213.  SHRI NAKUL KAMAL NATH: \n  \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) the names of the schemes of the Government which have been \nrenamed by the Government after 2014; and \n(b) the funds allocated to these schemes after 2014? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n (SHRI KAUSHAL KISHORE) \n \n(a) & (b) The Ministry had  launched National Urban Livelihoods \nMission (NULM), in 2014- 15, to reduce poverty and \nvulnerability of urban poor households on a sustainable basis \nin 790 cities. Since February 2016, the scheme has been \nrenamed as Deendayal Antyodaya Yojana – National Urb an \nLivelihoods Mission (DAY -NULM) and coverage has been \nextended to all statutory towns.  Under DAY-NULM, an amount \nof ₹5292.30 crore has been allocated since 2014- 15 till 30\nth \nJune, 2023. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_214": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 214 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON AUGUST 03, 2023 \nMANAGEMENT OF GARBAGE, WASTE AND SEWAGE \nNO. 214. SHRI DHANUSH M. KUMAR: \nSHRIMATI MANJULATA MANDAL: \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate?  \n(a) the aim of launching the National Capacity Building Framework  to \nachieve garbage free cities under Swachh Bharat Mission- Urban \n(SBM-U 2.0) along with funds allocated and utilized by States; and \n \n(b) the measures taken/being taken by the Government to make cities \ngarbage free? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS  \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) & (b): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 214 FOR 03.08.2023 REGARDING “MANAGEMENT OF \nGARBAGE, WASTE AND SEWAGE” \n(a) : The aim of launching the National Capacity Building \nFramework for Garbage Free Cities under Swachh Bharat \nMission-Urban (SBM-U 2.0) is:  \n \ni. To augment the capacity and skills of the existing cadre of \nofficials at State and ULBs  \n \nii. To strategically engage specialized human resource at  the \nState and ULB level to enhance the effectiveness and \nefficiency of Mission implementation \n \niii.  To strengthen e cosystem and institutionalizing capacity \nbuilding of States and ULBs through Centre of Excellence \nand Swachhta Knowledge Partners (SKPs)  \n \nDetails of the funds allocated and utilized in respect of National \nCapacity Building Framework to achieve garbage free cities by \nStates/UTs under SBM-U 2.0 is at Annexure-I. \n \n(b):   The following measures have been taken/are being taken by \nthe Government to make cities Garbage Free: \n \ni. Strengthened focus on 100% door to door collection of \nsegregated waste from each household.  \nii. Implementing segregation of waste at source of generation of \nwaste. \niii. Creating facilities for 100% scientific management of all \nfractions of waste such as Dry, Wet, Construction & \nDemolition waste.  \niv. Enhanced focus on remediation of all legacy waste dump \nsites. MoHUA has approved remediation of 1,785 dumpsites \nfor processing of 17.60 crore MT of legacy waste. The \napproved Central Financial Assistance for remediation of \nlegacy waste dumpsites is Rs. 3469 crore so far. \n\nv. Awareness creation along with large scale citizen outreach to \ncreate Jan-Andolan and institutionalize Swachh Behaviour. \nvi. All the cities have been encouraged to identify their gaps and \nprepare City Sanitation Action Plans, City Solid Waste Action \nPlans, Used Water Management Plans and IEC and Capacity \nBuilding Plans.  \nvii. Swachh Bharat Mission Urban 2.0 is being implemented with \nfinancial allocation of Rs. 1, 41,678 Cr. o ver a period of 5 \nyears 2021-26. The State wise allocation of funds under SBM -\nU 2.0 is at Annexure-II \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nReferred in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Starred Q. No. 214 (position 14 th) \ndue for reply on 03.08.2023 regarding “Management of Garbage, Waste and \nSewage” \n(Rs. in Crores) \nS. \nNo. State/ UT \nAllocation under \nSBM-U  \n(2014-2021) \nAllocation under \nSBM-U 2.0 \n(2021-2026) \nFunds Utilized \n1 A&N Islands 0.10 0.90 0.00 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 10.15 71.60 10.15 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 3.06 5.20 3.06 \n4 Assam 16.66 18.50 11.38 \n5 Bihar 11.91 53.30 9.75 \n6 Chandigarh 0.56 4.50 0.34 \n7 Chhattisgarh 10.23 31.30 10.23 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 0.10 0.70 \n0.01 \n9 Daman & Diu 0.13 0.00 \n10 Delhi 6.15 15.00 0.43 \n11 Goa 0.82 1.90 0.78 \n12 Gujarat 20.63 109.60 17.07 \n13 Haryana 7.60 35.40 5.30 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 2.76 5.70 1.53 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 3.68 20.60 3.47 \n16 Jharkhand 4.51 27.30 4.99 \n17 Karnataka 21.16 110.80 17.62 \n18 Kerala 5.30 32.20 2.42 \n19 Ladakh 1.58  3.00 0.00 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 25.56 96.50 25.56 \n21 Maharashtra 35.70 224.70 18.04 \n22 Manipu