��� ���������� ��� ���� ��������� ���������� ��������\n������ ������ ����������� ����� ����� ��������� ��� ��� ������� ���������� ���� �������� ����������\n������������� ����� ���� �������������� ��������������� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ���� ����� �����\n����������� ��� ���� ���������������������� ������� ��� ������ ���� ������������ ����������� ���\n������������������ ����������� ���� ��������� ��� ���� �������� ��� ������ �� �\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������������ ���������������������� �������� ������ ������ ���������� ���������� ������������������ ������������������ ���������������������� ������������������������\n������� ��������� ��������� ����������� ����������� ��� ������� ������� �������� ����� ������ ����\n���������� ��� ������� ���� ���� ��������� ��� ��� ��������� �������� ���� ����������� ���� �����\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��� ���� ����������� ������������ ��� ������� ����������� ������� ����� �������� ������������� ��� �����\n��������������� ��������� ��� ��������� ���� ��������� ��� ������� ������� ���� ��������� ��������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������\n������",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_49": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 49 \nANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 \n \nDelay in releasing AISHE Report \n \n*49. SHRI RAVIKUMAR D.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the reason for the inordinate delay in releasing the All India Survey on Higher Education \n(AISHE) 2020-21 report; and \n(b) whether the Government has taken any steps to release the AISHE Report and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) and (b): A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN RE PLY TO PART (a) and (b) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 49 ANSWERED ON 24.07.2023 ASKED BY \nSHRI RAVIKUMAR D., HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING \nDELAY IN RELEASING AISHE REPORT \n \n(a) The All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE) is the comprehensive source of \nHigher Education Statistics in the country.  Data on various parameters, such as \nEnrolment, Institution information, Teacher Information, etc are uploaded by Higher \nEducation I nstitutions (HEIs) registered under AISHE in the portal aishe.gov.in, in \nspecially designed formats of data collection. The AISHE Surveys have been \nconducted annually since 2011.  The AISHE Survey is conducted through the co -\noperation and support of the State Governments, Universities, etc.  \n \nThe Report of the 2019 -20 Survey and launch of the 2020 -21 Survey was affected by \nthe unprecedented global Covid  outbreak. The Academic Session 2020 -21 and \nAdmission process in the HEIs got considerably delayed, extending upto December, \n2020 and in a few cases even after that. The comprehensive data on course -\nwise/Programme-wise enrolments gets available in HEIs onl y after the admission \nprocess is fully completed. Further, the severe outbreak of 2 nd wave of Covid in 2021, \nin which staff of various Departments, State Governments and Universities got \naffected, also delayed the launch of the Survey.  \n \nIn AISHE 2020 -21, the data collection mechanism was upgraded from old system of \ndata filling in the Adobe Acrobat format, to a dynamic, user friendly and \ntechnologically upgraded Web Data Capture Format (DCF) platform by the National \nInformatics Centre (NIC).Till 2019 -20, t he Adobe software (windows 2008) was \nbeing used for data collection in AISHE which was hosted on physical server instead \nof cloud Server. Hence, for compliance with NIC IT infra -Policy and to upgrade the \nAISHE portal with new IT features, Web -DCF was intro duced in AISHE for \ncollecting the information during year 2021 and was used for first time in AISHE \n2020-21 Survey.  Consequently, functional testing was done followed by detailed \nSecurity Audit of the system and trainings to the State Nodal Officers/HEIs before \noperationalisation of new system.  \n \nBeing the first DCF Survey, the earlier data structure and reports had to be modified.  \nThis had to be tested/matched with the unit level data of the Survey.  In view of this, \nintense scrutiny was undertaken after closure of the Survey. \n   \n(b) The AISHE 2020 -21 Report, has already been released in January 2023.  It may be \nmentioned that the enrolment of the country has increased from 3.85 crore in 2019 -20 \nto 4.14 crore in 2020-21.   \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S12_U_5": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF DRINKING WATER & SANITATION \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 5  \n  (5th Position) \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20/07/2023 \n \nWorks under Atal Bhujal Yojana and Jal Jeevan Mission \n†*5. SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state: \n(a)  the works done by the Government under  the Atal Bhujal Yojana (ABY) and Jal Jeevan \nMission (JJM) in the country particularly in the States of  Maharashtra, Jharkhand, \nChhattisgarh, Rajasthan, Karnataka and North Eastern Region, district -wise along with the \ndetails of funds allocated and utilized in this regard; \n(b)  the efforts being made to speed up the  progress of work under the said scheme, State/  UT-\nwise particularly in Rajasthan; \n(c)  the number of households provided with tap  water connections under Har Ghar Jal Yojana \nunder JJM, State/ UT-wise;  \n(d)  whether complaints are being received about  irregularities and poor quality of works under \nthe JJM;  \n(e)  if so, the details of the works referred in such complaints, State/UT and district-wise; and  \n(f)  the list of the contractors identified and the details of the action taken on such defaulters? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT) \n (a) to (f) A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n\n2 \n \nStatement referred in reply of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 5 to be answered on \n20.07.2023 \n(a) to (c) Atal Bhujal Yojana is being implemented in water stressed Gram Panchayats (GPs) of \n229 administrative Blocks/ Talukas in 80 districts of 7 States, viz., Haryana, Gujarat, Karnataka, \nMadhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Rajasthan  and Uttar Pradesh for a period of 5 years from \n01.04.2020.  The scheme aims at community led sustainable ground water management throug h \nconvergence of activities.  \nThe scheme is being implemented through active participation of the communities in the \nparticipating States in various activities such as monitoring and disseminating ground water data, \nwater budgeting, preparation of Gram Pan chayat wise Water Security Plans having demand side \nas well as supply interventions proposed and their implementation through convergence of \nongoing schemes and IEC activities related to sustainable groundwater management. \nThe fund under the scheme, inter alia, are being re leased as per performance based upon \nachievements of certain pre -defined indicators. As on 12.07.2023, following funds have been \nreleased to 7 participating States   \n(Rs. in crore) \nFinancial Year Funds released \n2020-21 109.00 \n2021-22 310.40 \n2022-23 1,138.53 \n2023-24 130.97 \nTotal 1,688.90 \n \nVarious steps viz. optimization of parameters as per the Scheme objectives and Standard \nOperating Procedures for data collection by the stakeholders , modifications in MIS with an aim \nto make it more user friendly, regular reviews at various levels along with field visits by National \nProgram Monitoring Unit (NPMU) officers  etc. have been taken for expeditious implementation \nof the scheme. \nSince August 2019, Government of India, in partnership with Stat e Governments, is \nimplementing Jal Jeevan Mission (JJM) -Har Ghar Jal to enable every rural household in the \ncountry, including those in the States of Maharashtra, Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Rajasthan, \nKarnataka and North Eastern Region, to have assured potable water through tap connection. \nAt the time of announcement of Jal Jeevan Mission, 3.23 Crore (17%) rural households were \nreported to have tap water connections. So far, as reported by States/UTs as on 17.07.2023, \naround 9.35 Crore additional rural households have been pro vided with tap water connections \nunder JJM. Thus, as on 17.07.2023, out of 19.46 Crore rural households in the country, around \n12.58 Crore (64.6%) households are reported to have tap water supply in their homes.  State/ UT-\nwise and year-wise details are at Annex-I. \nDistrict-wise fund allocations and details thereof are not made and maintained by this \ndepartment. However, y ear-wise and State/ UT -wise details of Central fund allocated, fund \ndrawn and fund utilization reported by the States/ U Ts under Jal Jeeva n Mission in 2019 -20, \n2020-21, 2021-22, 2022-23 & 2023-24 (as on 17.07.2023) are at Annex-II. \n\n3 \n \nTo achieve the goal of JJM in the whole country with speed and scale, Government of India is \nhandholding the States,  including Rajasthan,  to exp edite the implemen tation of the mission \nthrough number of steps inter alia, which includes joint discussion and finalization of annual \naction plan (AAP) of States/ UTs , regular review of implementation, workshops/ conferences/ \nwebinars for capacity buildin g and knowledge sh aring, field visits by multi -disciplinary team to \nprovide technical support, etc. A detailed Operational Guideline for the implementation of JJM; \nMargdarshika for Gram Panchayats & VWSCs to provide safe drinking water in rural \nhouseholds and Guidelines on a special campaign to provide piped water supply in anganwadi \ncentres, ashramshalas and schools have been shared with States/ UTs to facilitate planning and \nimplementation of Jal Jeevan Mission. For online monitoring, JJM –Integrated Manag ement \nInformation System (IMIS) and JJM –Dashboard has been put in place. Provision has also been \nmade for transparent online financial management through Public Financial Management System \n(PFMS). \n(d) to (f) Drinking Water being a State subject, it is Stat es, who plan, desi gn, approve and \nimplement drinking water supply schemes. Government of India supplements the efforts of the \nStates by providing technical and financial assistance. As such details of individual projects/ \nschemes which inter alia include number and names of agencies involved for implementation of \nrural water supply projects are not maintained at the Government of India level. Further, \ngrievances/ complaints etc. inter alia including sub-standard work and irregularities are handled \nand disposed at State/ UT l evel. Such matters/ representations thus received in this Department \nso far have been forwarded to the respective State Governments for taking necessary action. \n*****\n\n4 \n \nAnnex-I \nAnnex-1 referred to in Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 5 to be answered on 20.07.2023 \nState/ UT-wise status of tap water connections in rural households \n(as on 17.07.2023) \n(Number in lakhs) \nS. \nNo. State/ UT \nTotal \nrural HHs \nas on date \nRural HHs with \ntap water \nconnection as on \nRural HHs given tap water connections Rural HHs with \ntap water supply \nNo. In % 19-20 20-21 21-22 22-23 23-24 Total In % No. In% \n1. A & N Islands 0.62 0.29 46.02  -     0.33   -     -     -     0.33   53.98   0.62   100.00  \n2. Andhra Pr. 95.55 30.74 32.18  1.19   12.77   9.59   12.12   0.52   36.19   37.87   66.93   70.05  \n3. Arunachal Pr. 2.30 0.23 9.90  0.13   0.65   0.42   0.34   0.17   1.72   74.50   1.94   84.40  \n4. Assam 68.21 1.11 1.63  0.49   5.07   16.52   8.79   3.50   34.37   50.39   35.48   52.02  \n5. Bihar 166.30 3.16 1.90  28.97   103.46   19.86   3.63   1.21   157.12   94.48   160.29   96.38  \n6. Chhattisgarh 50.11 3.20 6.38  0.96   1.51   4.45   10.81   5.67   23.41   46.71   26.60   53.09  \n7. DNH and DD 0.85 0.00 0.00  -     0.25   0.61   -     -     0.85   100.00   0.85   100.00  \n8. Goa 2.63 1.99 75.70  0.31   0.33   -     -     -     0.64   24.30   2.63   100.00  \n9. Gujarat 91.18 65.16 71.46  1.06   10.95   8.99   5.03   -     26.02   28.54   91.18   100.00  \n10. Haryana 30.41 17.66 58.08  1.35   7.91   3.49   -     -     12.75   41.92   30.41   100.00  \n11. Himachal Pr. 17.09 7.63 44.64  1.59   3.79   2.87   0.93   0.29   9.46   55.36   17.09   100.00  \n12. J&K 18.66 5.75 30.84  2.07   2.16   0.58   0.40   1.34   6.55   35.08   12.30   65.91  \n13. Jharkhand 61.23 3.45 5.64  0.95   